1. Structural checks (do these first — before touching any values)

 Check shape (df.shape) — does row/column count look sane?
 Check dtypes (df.info()) — is every column the type you expect? (a numeric column showing as object means something non-numeric snuck in)
 Check column names — consistent casing, no stray whitespace, no duplicate column names

2. Find hidden/disguised missing values (do this before .isna() checks mean anything)

 Scan for string placeholders masquerading as data: "NA", "N/A", "null", "none", "-", "?", "unknown", "missing", empty string ""
 Scan numeric columns for sentinel values used instead of real blanks: 0, -1, 999, 9999 sitting suspiciously outside the normal range
 Convert any of the above into real NaN so pandas can actually track them — don't clean around them while they're still disguised as strings

3. Duplicates

 Check for fully duplicate rows (df.duplicated().sum())
 Check for duplicates ignoring the ID column (in case the ID was slightly altered but everything else matches)
 Check for duplicate IDs with different data (a different kind of problem — possible data-entry conflict, not a simple duplicate)

4. Categorical consistency

 For every categorical column, run .unique() and look for: casing differences, leading/trailing whitespace, typos, synonyms meaning the same thing
 Standardise each one to a single canonical spelling/casing
 Check for categories that shouldn't logically exist (e.g. a "gender" value that's actually a typo'd age)

5. Numeric plausibility

 Check .describe() min/max for every numeric column — do the extremes make physical/logical sense? (negative age, BMI over 80, blood pressure of 0)
 Decide per-column: drop the row, or is there a defensible correction (not a guess)
 Check for values with implausible precision or formatting inconsistency (e.g. some ages as floats, some as ints)

6. Now — and only now — assess real missing values

 Run .isna().sum() per column (this is now meaningful, since step 2 converted disguised missing values into real NaNs)
 Decide, per column, whether the missing count is manageable to drop, or too large and needs a different strategy
 Confirm your decision doesn't wipe out a column entirely (if 90% of a column is missing, dropping rows isn't viable — that's a design decision to flag, not silently drop)

7. Cross-column logical consistency (often skipped, worth doing)

 Check for logically impossible combinations across columns (e.g. smoking_status = "Never" but a pack_years column > 0, if such a column existed)
 Check that categorical relationships make sense (e.g. region values are all from an expected fixed set, no stray new categories appearing once cleaned)

8. Final sanity pass

 Re-run .info() and .describe() on the cleaned result
 Confirm dtypes are correct (no lingering object columns that should be numeric)
 Confirm no NaNs remain if your goal is a fully complete baseline
 Record final row/column count vs. original, and % of data retained